1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,230
This is ScreenToStream Concierge. It turns the show you're watching into a bookable weekend.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,420
The simulator is a real MCP client. It completes OAuth 2.1 with PKCE before calling Alexa Plus.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,800
Book a weekend stay here next spring.

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:23,210
The server resolves Kyoto, builds a two-night stay, and sends a card to the TV.

5
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:31,100
The guest can refine in natural language. Pricing stays deterministic, so the quote is instant.

6
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,800
The quote is held, then confirmed.

7
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:39,500
The server creates a simulated booking and exports a calendar file.

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,800
After confirmation, it creates a simulated booking,

9
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,100
sends the itinerary back to the TV, and exports a calendar file.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,450
The card lands back on the TV.

11
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:59,000
Under the hood: MCP, Streamable HTTP, fourteen tools,

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,390
OAuth discovery, and the bare 401 Alexa Plus expects.

13
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:10,200
Bedrock's Converse tool loop is the agent: it picks one of fourteen MCP tools,

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:16,800
calls the MCP endpoint, and feeds the result back until the trip is planned.

15
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,500
The adapter test passes; make bedrock-check is the live gate.

16
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,300
This render uses the deterministic fallback.

17
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,410
The show ends. The trip begins. ScreenToStream Concierge.
